          "title": "1. Projectiles Are Two Problems",
          "sequenceOrder": 1,
          "status": "published",
          "xpReward": 60,
          "maxReattempts": 3,
          "passingThreshold": 60,
          "estimatedDuration": 11,
          "difficulty": "HARD",
          "learnBlocks": [
            {
              "id": "al-1-1",
              "type": "blob_dialog",
              "content": "A projectile question is two independent problems that happen to share a clock.",
              "metadata": {
                "version": 1,
                "lines": [
                  {
                    "id": "l1",
                    "text": "A projectile question is two independent problems that happen to share a clock.",
                    "mood": "thinking",
                    "cta": "Independent?"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "l2",
                    "text": "Horizontally there is no acceleration, so the horizontal velocity never changes.",
                    "mood": "happy",
                    "cta": "And vertically?"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "l3",
                    "text": "Vertically it is free fall at g. The only thing the two share is the time.",
                    "mood": "cheer",
                    "cta": "That is the whole trick"
                  }
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "al-1-2",
              "type": "formula",
              "content": "x = u\\cos\\theta \\cdot t \\qquad y = u\\sin\\theta \\cdot t - \\tfrac{1}{2}gt^2"
            },
            {
              "id": "al-1-3",
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Resolve at the start, solve each direction separately, and let **time** be the bridge between them. Most lost marks come from mixing the two directions into one equation."
            },
            {
              "id": "al-1-4",
              "type": "callout",
              "content": "At the highest point the vertical velocity is zero, but the horizontal velocity is unchanged. The speed there is not zero."
            }
          ],
          "evaluateBlocks": [
            {
              "id": "al-eb-1-1",
              "type": "order_steps",
              "question": "Put the projectile method in the order that keeps the directions separate.",
              "correctAnswer": "resolve>vertical>horizontal>combine",
              "explanation": "Time comes from the vertical motion and is then carried across to the horizontal, which is what keeps the two problems separate.",
              "metadata": {
                "version": 1,
                "steps": [
                  {
                    "id": "resolve",
                    "label": "Resolve the launch velocity into components"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "vertical",
                    "label": "Use the vertical equation to find the time"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "horizontal",
                    "label": "Put that time into the horizontal equation"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "combine",
                    "label": "Combine the components for the final answer"
                  }
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "al-eb-1-2",
              "type": "tap_target",
              "question": "At the top of its flight, what is true of a projectile launched at an angle?",
              "correctAnswer": "horizontal-only",
              "explanation": "The vertical component is momentarily zero, but the horizontal component has not changed since launch.",
              "metadata": {
                "version": 1,
                "targets": [
                  {
                    "id": "horizontal-only",
                    "label": "Its velocity is horizontal and non-zero"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "zero",
                    "label": "Its velocity is zero"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "vertical-only",
                    "label": "Its velocity is vertical"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "max-speed",
                    "label": "Its speed is at a maximum"
                  }
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "al-eb-1-3",
              "type": "toggle_switch",
              "question": "Switch on the statements that hold for ideal projectile motion.",
              "correctAnswer": "horizontal-constant=on,shared-time=on,vertical-constant=off",
              "explanation": "With no air resistance the horizontal velocity is constant and the vertical one changes at g, while a single time links them.",
              "metadata": {
                "version": 1,
                "switches": [
                  {
                    "id": "horizontal-constant",
                    "label": "The horizontal velocity is constant",
                    "onLabel": "holds",
                    "offLabel": "does not hold"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "vertical-constant",
                    "label": "The vertical velocity is constant",
                    "onLabel": "holds",
                    "offLabel": "does not hold"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "shared-time",
                    "label": "Both directions share the same time of flight",
                    "onLabel": "holds",
                    "offLabel": "does not hold"
                  }
                ]
              }
            }
          ]
