          "title": "1. EMF and Internal Resistance",
          "sequenceOrder": 1,
          "status": "published",
          "xpReward": 60,
          "maxReattempts": 3,
          "passingThreshold": 60,
          "estimatedDuration": 11,
          "difficulty": "HARD",
          "learnBlocks": [
            {
              "id": "al-4-1",
              "type": "blob_dialog",
              "content": "A real cell cannot deliver its full EMF, because it resists its own current.",
              "metadata": {
                "version": 1,
                "lines": [
                  {
                    "id": "l1",
                    "text": "A real cell cannot deliver its full EMF, because it resists its own current.",
                    "mood": "thinking",
                    "cta": "Internal resistance?"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "l2",
                    "text": "Exactly. The terminal voltage is the EMF minus the volts lost inside the cell.",
                    "mood": "happy",
                    "cta": "So it drops under load?"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "l3",
                    "text": "The bigger the current, the bigger the loss, which is why headlights dim when the starter turns.",
                    "mood": "cheer",
                    "cta": "That explains it"
                  }
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "al-4-2",
              "type": "formula",
              "content": "V_{terminal} = \\varepsilon - Ir"
            },
            {
              "id": "al-4-3",
              "type": "ohms_law_lab",
              "content": "Treat the resistance slider as the external load. A smaller load draws more current, and a real cell would sag under it.",
              "metadata": {
                "version": 1,
                "voltage": 12,
                "resistance": 4,
                "maxVoltage": 12,
                "maxResistance": 12,
                "caption": "The bench is ideal, so nothing sags here. That gap is exactly what internal resistance accounts for."
              }
            }
          ],
          "evaluateBlocks": [
            {
              "id": "al-eb-4-1",
              "type": "tap_target",
              "question": "A cell of EMF 6.0 V and internal resistance 0.5 ohm delivers 2.0 A. What is the terminal voltage?",
              "correctAnswer": "five",
              "explanation": "Terminal voltage = 6.0 - (2.0 x 0.5) = 5.0 V.",
              "metadata": {
                "version": 1,
                "targets": [
                  {
                    "id": "five",
                    "label": "5.0 V"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "seven",
                    "label": "7.0 V"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "six",
                    "label": "6.0 V"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "three",
                    "label": "3.0 V"
                  }
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "al-eb-4-2",
              "type": "toggle_switch",
              "question": "Switch on what happens as the external load resistance falls.",
              "correctAnswer": "higher-emf=off,lower-terminal=on,more-current=on",
              "explanation": "EMF is a property of the cell and does not change. A smaller load draws more current, and the internal drop grows with it.",
              "metadata": {
                "version": 1,
                "switches": [
                  {
                    "id": "more-current",
                    "label": "The current drawn rises",
                    "onLabel": "true",
                    "offLabel": "false"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "lower-terminal",
                    "label": "The terminal voltage falls",
                    "onLabel": "true",
